 # Hierarchical Grid Optimization
 
+Also called: Explicit Radiance Fields Optimization, Plenoxels Optimization, Coarse-to-Fine Optimization, PlenOctree Optimization, Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization, Multi-scale Neural Field Reconstruction
 
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Grid Optimization is a family of differentiable volume rendering methods that represent scenes using explicit multi-resolution voxel grids or octrees, optimizing density and color parameters directly without neural networks. It solves the computational bottleneck of implicit neural field training by leveraging analytical gradients and iterative linear solvers (such as Gauss-Newton) or efficient sparse optimization, enabling significantly faster convergence and real-time rendering capabilities [S1, S2, S4, S5]. It sets itself apart from implicit methods like [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) by replacing the neural network with a sparse 3D voxel grid storing density and spherical harmonic (SH) coefficients, trading increased memory consumption for significant gains in efficiency [S1, unofficial][S8, unofficial].
+Hierarchical Grid Optimization refers to a family of differentiable volume rendering methods that replace implicit neural networks with explicit, multi-resolution 3D data structures (voxel grids, octrees, or pyramids) to optimize scene representation. It solves the computational bottleneck and high memory usage of neural field optimization by leveraging analytical gradients, iterative linear solvers, or efficient sparse data structures [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) which rely on MLP inference for every sample, this family optimizes discrete grid parameters (density, color, spherical harmonics) directly, enabling training speeds orders of magnitude faster than NeRF and real-time rendering capabilities [S1, unofficial][S6, unofficial].
 
